These are eight of the primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which links all of its sources.

The PHP Foundation's post covers the release and the embargo. Calif's write-up covers the discovery. Amazon's post covers the attempt before disclosure. Tenable explains why one exploit does not work everywhere. Wiz's post covers exploitation and remediation trends; as the page discloses, the site's author works at Wiz. Dream, Unit 42 and Palo Alto Networks cover the PAN-OS campaign and advisory.

Left out here, and linked on the page: PHP's three advisories and release announcement; Daniel Scherzer's timeline; the PHP2Shell disclosure site; Wiz's research post; guidance from AWS, Microsoft, Google Cloud and Cloudflare; notices from CISA, the NSA, the FBI, the UK NCSC and NCSC-NL, and NCSC-NL's information repository; Oracle and Sophos advisories; analyses by Adel Karimi, SOCRadar, CYFIRMA, Arctic Wolf, Datadog, Qualys and Tenable's FAQ; and coverage by The Register, The Record, SecurityWeek, BankInfoSecurity, BleepingComputer, The Hacker News and eBuilder Security.

The background slides name their own sources, from OWASP and MITRE ATT&CK.
